Date: Fri, 18 Feb 94 00:14:29 GMT
Subject: [#94-2-162] SCI:Femtotech

I was intrigued by January 94's Scientific American article _The Search
for Strange Matter_.  It discusses the possibility of the strange quark
allowing more complex nuclear structures (strangelets) than we normally
find.  Atomic nuclei are an approx even mixture of up and down quarks,
packaged up as  nucleons (protons and neutrons).  Aggregates of up and
down quarks carry a high charge mass ratio of approx 1:2 - each up quark
carries a 2/3 charge and each down quark carries a -1/3 charge.  Large
atomic nuclei (amu > 207) are unstable and eventually (>263?) blown
apart by their net positive charge.

The strange (or sideways) quark, like the down quark, also carries a -
1/3 charge.  Strange quarks are heavier than up or down quarks so there
will be slightly fewer of them in any strangelet mixture.  Calculations
are imprecise (QCD!) but it seems that mixtures of up, down and strange
quarks will have a much lower charge mass ratio, somewhere between +/-
1:10 - +/-1:20.  Result: large clumps of strangelet matter may be
stable.

Some theorists have predicted that strangelet quark balls were formed
in the first 10^-6s of the universe's history.  These quark balls are
very heavy but very small and so almost invisible to astronomers,
perhaps contributing to the hypothesised missing or dark mass.  They
range from 10^33 quarks/10^-9m diameter/10^6 kg to 10^42 quarks/0.1 m
diameter/10^15 kg and impact on the production of helium-4 and other
light isotopes.  Observed values rule out any primordial quark nuggets
with a size of less than 10^23 quarks.  (Other calculations give wider
ranges for stable structures.)

A strangelet chunk dropping onto neutron star would catalyse the
conversion of the neutron star into strangelet matter within minutes. 
The ex-neutron star's radius would contract significantly as the quarks
bind more tightly together - speeding up its rotation to beyond the rate
a neutron star might sustain without being torn apart.  Astronomers are
searching for such rapidly spinning strange stars.

Other scientists are searching for smaller strangelets trapped in
meteorite material by bombarding them with high-energy uranium nuclei. 
180 degree Rutherford deflections will indicate the presence of embedded
massive strangelets, acting as super-heavy nuclei.

Any relevance to extropians?  Perhaps not.  But I like to imagine that
nanotech is not the end of the road.  If strangelet quark structures
occur naturally, and perhaps even if they don't, then we have the
possibility of one day constructing femtomachines with atomic nuclear
densities, engineered down on the femtoscale, 10^-15m.  At least we
mustn't think of nanotech as the "last technological revolution" as EOC
so boldly and entropically declares.
