Subject: [#94-2-166] PCR: Creation Ex Nihilo

A few comments on Steve Whitrow's post on many-worlds.

Steve talks about many-worlds and matter and antimatter universes
cancelling out.  It's important to bear in mind that many-worlds is an
interpretation of quantum theory, which itself is a theory of a single
physical reality.  Most physicists believe that the matter-antimatter
asymmetry in the universe stems from the laws of physics.  Virtually all
the simultaneously parallel worlds required by the many-worlds
interpretation of QM will have the same preponderance of matter over
antimatter.  Even if not so, matter and antimatter worlds wouldn't
cancel out in quantum theory.

To avoid confusion when talking about the MWI of QM over "worlds" and
"universes" I prefer to stick to the following distinction
(unfortunately there is no consensus in the literature which is
absolutely infuriating).  In the MWI of QM all the elements (worlds) of
the superposition form a single physical reality (the universe). 
Physical theory deals with the universe, which is why we are able to
infer the existence of these other worlds.  Our perception(s) is(are)
limited to single worlds.

Other universes may exist, and some may be governed by different laws -
 if the term "exist" has any meaning here, which it may not!  I don't
see how we can ever make any inferences about these other universes -
although this might just be a limitation of my sub-jupiter brain.  Since
it seems likely we can never physically interact with other universes
the notion of matter and antimatter universes has no meaning.

I think Steve is using the term "multiverse" to refer to the totality
of all universes, some which may have different laws.  A good use of the
term and one I shall adopt.

Some minor nitpicks:
> Maybe there are universes of infinite age and entropy, but they
> certainly can't contain lifeforms.  

Some recent eternal inflation models don't have a big-bang and posit a
universe that is infinitely old (eternal).  On the very large scale the
universe inflates and always has and always will.  The observable
universe is a single non-inflating bubble that forms within the wider
expansion.  Infinite age and infinite total entropy, although finite
entropy density.

> And other finite[ly old] universes, also lifeless, again have no
> observers to confirm their existence.  They may be considered to
> exist somewhere in reality space as theoretical possible permutations,
> but have a zero reality coefficient, unlike life-bearing permutations.

Linking "reality coefficients" to the existence of life is not a good
idea.  The universe is older than we are!  What's the problem with 
universes existing devoid of life?
