>argument that it's the only alternative (anyone has thought of) to
>something much worse--some one person or small group taking over
>everything.

?  If not, you must be basing your pro-blue-goo stance on some change you
forsee relative to the prior status quo.  But given the risks in trying
blue goo, I want to hear a much more coherent argument about why the
future will be so different from the past, and different in just the way
that makes blue goo worth considering.  General references to how things
will change, and change faster, are not enough for me.

Paul Cisek writes:
>Actually, my point is more a mathematical one.  I don't claim to have any
