Subject: [#94-2-177] PCR: Creation Ex Nihilo

Mike Price replies:
>Steve talks about many-worlds and matter and antimatter universes
>cancelling out.  It's important to bear in mind that many-worlds is an
>interpretation of quantum theory, which itself is a theory of a single
>physical reality.

I've sometimes used "Many Worlds / Many Minds" to refer to what might be
termed the "family" of many worlds-type interpretations.  For example,
there is Many Worlds, Many Minds, and my interpretation which may or may
not have minor differences with Many Minds.  I'm rather confident that
the correct interpretation comes from this family, as most of us on this
list agree, and would give it a PCR probability of around 0.9999 (as
opposed to 0.55 to 0.8 which I'd give to some of my ideas).  In future,
I think I'll describe my model as "Many Realities".

(I don't wish to develop a model which gives alternative predictions,
just another way of visualising the whole thing that makes more sense to
me, and seems a more logical model.)

>I think Steve is using the term "multiverse" to refer to the totality
>of all universes, some which may have different laws.  A good use of the
>term and one I shall adopt.

Not quite.  I use "omniverse" for the totality of all universes (including
those devoid of life and with different laws), and "multiverse" for the
myriad of universes occupied by a given mind at a given instant.  As each
individual continues to diverge (and converge), hir mind occupies
different multiverses at different times.  In fact, probably no two
individuals would ever straddle exactly the same set of universes, but
there would be enough of an overlap, with branches occuring as required,
to prevent any odd effects.  E.g. observer #1 finds that a cat is alive,
observer #2 is unaware of the cat's state and still occupies those
universes where #1 has just found the cat to be dead.  As soon as #1
communicates the findings to #2, #2 and #1(dead) are totally separated.

>In the MWI of QM all the elements (worlds) of
>the superposition form a single physical reality (the universe). 
>Physical theory deals with the universe, which is why we are able to
>infer the existence of these other worlds.  Our perception(s) is(are)
>limited to single worlds.

I just use "universes", "worlds" and "(permutations of) realit(y)ies" as
synonyms.  In a spatial and temporal sense these worlds are strictly in a
parallel superposition, although each possible permutation of reality is
in a different point in "reality space".  So in your MWI parlance these
would be elements or worlds, and the single physical reality (universe)
is something between a multiverse and the omniverse.  In fact it would
encompass all the worlds occupied by humans' minds, and possibly some
containing aliens, and others where life never took root.

>  Most physicists believe that the matter-antimatter
>asymmetry in the universe stems from the laws of physics.  Virtually all
>the simultaneously parallel worlds required by the many-worlds
>interpretation of QM will have the same preponderance of matter over
>antimatter.  Even if not so, matter and antimatter worlds wouldn't
>cancel out in quantum theory.

In the multiverses of each human, virtually all worlds have far more
matter than antimatter.  The antimatter universes are inaccessible to us,
outside of all of our multiverses and outside of MWI's "single physical
reality" (but your antimatter twin is reading this at this very moment!).
I posit the existence of these antimatter universes partly because the
extra symmetry makes the model more elegant, and partly because it helps
with the problem of creation ex nihilo -- nothing was ever created in an
absolute sense, since it was immediately annuled by its opposite.  This
may be overkill, as we already have the +ve matter energy vs. -ve
gravitational energy balance.

But since these antimatter universes may in principle exist, and our
antimatter twins would be equally convinced that their universes are
real, wouldn't it be arrogant of us to claim that we have a monopoly on
reality?  In fact, these antimatter universes contain life, and so in a
sense are more "real" than lifeless matter universes.

>Linking "reality coefficients" to the existence of life is not a good
>idea.  The universe is older than we are!  What's the problem with 
>universes existing devoid of life?

None at all, and almost all possible universes would be dead.  I take the
view that "it can exist, therefore it does."  Although it does appear that
even with MWI you may get "I can exist, therefore I am", with implications
for an immortal soul.  I think one answer to this is that according to MWI
there are only something like 10^10^150 possible worlds before you have to
include those with different laws, so it does make sense to attempt to
accentuate the positive, or you don't get an accurate identity continuity.

But regarding reality coefficients in life-bearing universes, at first
sight this provides another creation solution.  Not only do you have
equal opposites, but the RC of each matter / antimatter universe pair
against time forms elegant +ve and -ve exponential curves.  No sudden
bumps to explain, RC is just proportional to the number of conscious
observers times their mean level of consciousness.

However, these life-bearing universes often go on to evolve advanced or
semi-advanced lifeforms who observe the Big Bang background radiation,
analyse the distribution of particles and start computing what happened
in the first 10^-43 second.  There is a sudden bump in the RC after all.
Apply this at the multiverse level and you've got something like John
Wheeler's "participatory universe".  The Big Bang was real, those beings
in the future observed it.  And reality gradually evolves further, with
the discovery of each new planet for example bringing it into existence.  

I'd be satisfied with having all possible universes existing in some
sense, but not in an absolute sense, with those containing life at a
distinct advantage in the reality stakes.  Maybe I should scrap the
reality coefficient concept altogether -- the zero RC for dead universes
certainly needs adjustment.  Let's make it 1 + (quantity of conscious
observers times their mean level of consciousness), and if antimatter
then multiply by -1.

