On Thu, 17 Feb 1994, Jay R. Freeman wrote:

> Stephen J. Whitrow responds to vaald@westminster.ac.uk:
> 
> > Rights may arise from superior firepower, superior cunning, harnesses, or
> > sheer outnumbering, but [...]
> 
>   The Squirrel begs to point out that rights may also arise from the
>   persuasive powers of superior intelligence and considered appeals to
>   enlightened reasoning, though possibly not among humans.

I'd like to point out (mainly for my own understanding) that
"rights" seem to require at least two people (or squirrels, whatever
the case may be) to be recognized. And even then, there must be
a good reason to mutually adopt said "right".

Having said that, I'm not sure how a "right" could arise simply
by superior intelligence and persuasive powers, unless everyone
was at the same level of intelligence (assuming this is what
is required to understand the proposed "right"), or even how
persuasive powers have anything to do with it besides effectively
bullshitting your message across (in which case I propose that
eventually either everyone will come to see your way, or everyone
will realise it was just BS...)

I can only see a generally accepted "right" arising (I prefer to
say it's coming into use) where it's widely accepted as being
beneficial to use it (I'm using beneficial in a self-oriented
way here). I think this may be a reason why many are seeing the
erosion of the Bill of Right's in the US, without much of a
fight, since many people believe that it is in their better
interest to let them go (God knows we have to get rid of those
evil drug kingpins selling joints to your innocent children...)

Or maybe squirrels know something I don't...

